WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Four Artemis two astronauts accepted the Stephen Hawking Medal for turning their lunar flight into a shared human experience. This is Fascinating Frontiers Episode 226. Read Wiseman and Victor Glover of the National Aeronautics and Space Administration, or NASA, joined Canadian Space Agency astronaut Jeremy Hanson on stage at Starmus 8 in Tenerife on October 17th. Kristina Koch was also recognized even though she could not attend, according to Space.com. The award honors their lives

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:35.300
communications, interviews, and photos that let people worldwide follow the mission in real time.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:40.580
Weisman described seeing Earth the size of a thumb from 400,000 kilometers away

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:46.020
and feeling only love, peace, hope, and unity. He closed by telling the audience that

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:51.200
everyone can choose to make tomorrow better. Previous metal recipients include Buzz Aldrin,

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:57.080
Jane Goodall, and David Attenborough. Starmas co-founder Brian May noted the special thrill of

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:01.560
honoring the Artemis II crew in the same way the Apollo astronauts were honored more than

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:02.700
50 years earlier.

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:07.920
The festival is running under the theme, The Search for Truth, and organizers highlighted

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:12.760
how the astronauts' public engagement aligned with the metal's goal of making science accessible

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:13.620
and inspiring.

00:01:14.582 --> 00:01:19.122
New York University researchers built a random positioning machine to rotate living

00:01:19.122 --> 00:01:22.042
cell cultures and remove the constant pull of gravity.

00:01:22.042 --> 00:01:27.962
After 24 hours, the nuclei in the simulated microgravity samples grew larger than those

00:01:27.962 --> 00:01:29.362
kept under normal conditions.

00:01:29.942 --> 00:01:31.222
Universe today reported.

00:01:31.862 --> 00:01:36.462
The nuclear envelope thickness stayed the same, even as overall nuclear volume increased.

00:01:37.142 --> 00:01:41.802
The team also developed three-dimensional algorithms that reduced unwanted fluid flows

00:01:41.802 --> 00:01:46.642
during the rotation, allowing clearer observation of genome organization changes.

00:01:52.882 --> 00:01:58.802
The experiment used advanced imaging to track cell and nuclear responses under each condition

00:01:58.802 --> 00:02:04.982
and recorded streams of images for detailed analysis of genome dynamics and possible DNA damage.

00:02:05.422 --> 00:02:10.722
The random positioning machine followed a three-dimensional path to create the microgravity simulation,

00:02:11.142 --> 00:02:15.462
while the team minimized cell aggregation that had obscured results in prior work.

00:02:15.922 --> 00:02:16.622
The study built up a three-dimensional system to make sure that the system was able

00:02:16.622 --> 00:02:21.262
built on earlier findings from NASA's Twin Study, and Zadavska explained that gravity

00:02:21.262 --> 00:02:26.642
acts as a constant mechanical stress, whose removal reveals how the genome organizes itself

00:02:26.642 --> 00:02:27.602
without that force.

00:02:28.824 --> 00:02:34.084
Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory researchers ran more than 1,500 one-dimensional

00:02:34.084 --> 00:02:37.884
sphero simulations to match results from their established Aries code.

00:02:38.484 --> 00:02:44.304
They then modeled three disruption scenarios for a 160-meter irregular asteroid, varying

00:02:44.304 --> 00:02:48.944
detonation distance and material fracture strength, according to Fizz.org.

00:02:49.464 --> 00:02:55.964
In the longest run, 98% of the asteroid material became fully damaged and 97% exceeded escape

00:02:55.964 --> 00:02:56.524
velocity.

00:02:57.224 --> 00:03:05.104
The longest simulation used 1,680 processors for 59 days, yet advanced only 145 milliseconds

00:03:05.104 --> 00:03:06.064
of physical time.

00:03:06.844 --> 00:03:11.964
Isaiah Santistevan, who led the study, noted that the three-dimensional view reveals how internal

00:03:11.964 --> 00:03:17.384
strength variations and detonation geometry alter fragment outcomes. Two of the three

00:03:17.384 --> 00:03:22.264
scenarios produce strong disruption signatures, with material moving in opposing directions

00:03:22.264 --> 00:03:27.444
after the simulated nuclear event. The team validated the spiral approach by comparing

00:03:27.444 --> 00:03:31.884
outputs against established deflection models before moving to full disruption cases.

00:03:32.504 --> 00:03:37.204
Placing the energy deposit farther from the surface can spread damage across more of the body

00:03:37.204 --> 00:03:39.564
even when the total energy delivered is lower.

00:03:40.404 --> 00:03:42.924
Three-dimensional modeling captures fracture patterns

00:03:42.924 --> 00:03:45.784
and fragment motion that one-dimensional codes miss.

00:03:46.344 --> 00:03:48.444
The models track damage, fracture,

00:03:48.684 --> 00:03:50.304
and material motion simultaneously,

00:03:50.964 --> 00:03:52.344
giving a more complete picture

00:03:52.344 --> 00:03:54.084
than earlier one-dimensional runs.

00:03:55.089 --> 00:03:57.489
The next modeling step is to follow those fragments

00:03:57.489 --> 00:03:59.569
for longer times so their ultimate paths

00:03:59.569 --> 00:04:01.089
and sizes can be predicted.

00:04:01.829 --> 00:04:04.549
Santa Stefan emphasized that the high fidelity approach

00:04:04.549 --> 00:04:09.669
which improves evaluation of planetary defense options by showing realistic material responses.

00:04:10.189 --> 00:04:15.189
The open modeling question is what size threshold would make disruption preferable to deflection

00:04:15.189 --> 00:04:16.489
for a given warning time.

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:22.390
Jets near Enceladus's South Pole spray water vapor and icy particles from the subsurface

00:04:22.390 --> 00:04:24.110
ocean through kilometers of ice.

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:29.190
Salt crystals in those particles reach the surface and could preserve chemical clues about

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:32.670
the hidden ocean without any drilling, Fizz.org reported.

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:37.770
The particles offer a direct sample of material that has interacted with the Moon's interior.

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:43.530
The ocean lies beneath an ice shell that prevents direct access, yet the plumes provide a natural

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:45.210
pathway for material to escape.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:50.050
Scientists can examine the salt crystals for dissolved minerals that record conditions

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:51.010
in the water below.

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:56.010
This approach avoids the need for a lander or drill and instead uses the Moon's own

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:57.550
activity to deliver samples.

00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:03.255
A moon only a few hundred kilometers across still hides liquid water kept from freezing

00:05:03.255 --> 00:05:06.315
by tidal heating generated during its orbit around Saturn.

00:05:06.755 --> 00:05:12.015
That same flexing opens fractures at the South Pole, allowing pressurized water to erupt

00:05:12.015 --> 00:05:15.355
outward in plumes that climb hundreds of kilometers into space.

00:05:15.875 --> 00:05:20.515
Because the spray originates directly from the hidden sea, each particle carries a chemical

00:05:20.515 --> 00:05:22.575
signature of the water it left behind.

00:05:22.575 --> 00:05:27.575
Salt crystals form inside the grains when liquid evaporates or freezes rapidly.

00:05:28.195 --> 00:05:32.915
Those crystals trapped dissolved minerals that record temperature, acidity, and the possible

00:05:32.915 --> 00:05:34.515
presence of organic compounds.

00:05:35.235 --> 00:05:40.595
Earlier spacecraft measurements already detected sodium, potassium, chlorine, and simple

00:05:40.595 --> 00:05:42.335
organics within the plume material.

00:05:42.875 --> 00:05:46.355
Those readings averaged many particles over short fly-through windows.

00:05:47.035 --> 00:05:51.735
Future collectors could separate individual grains, dissolve the salts, and run them

00:05:51.735 --> 00:05:56.555
through mass spectrometers or microscopes to reconstruct the ocean's full chemical profile.

00:05:57.375 --> 00:06:01.835
Observers can watch the plumes erupt in real-time from orbit, yet the duration of the

00:06:01.835 --> 00:06:04.215
ocean's exchange with the surface remains unknown.

00:06:04.935 --> 00:06:09.115
It is still unknown whether that exchange ever created conditions suitable for biology

00:06:09.115 --> 00:06:09.695
to start.

00:06:10.235 --> 00:06:15.095
The open question is whether the salts and organics preserve active chemical gradients that might

00:06:15.095 --> 00:06:19.915
support life, or whether they reflect only ancient, unchanging conditions sealed away

00:06:19.915 --> 00:06:20.535
long ago.

00:06:20.535 --> 00:06:27.162
China's fast radio telescope detected a compact cloud of neutral hydrogen with no visible stars.

00:06:27.502 --> 00:06:31.962
The cloud contains nearly 500,000 times the sun's mass in hydrogen,

00:06:32.522 --> 00:06:35.682
and shows no optical counterpart according to Fizz.org.

00:06:36.042 --> 00:06:40.422
The discovery paper appeared on the archive pre-print server on September 24.

00:06:40.842 --> 00:06:45.322
The object stands out because it lacks any detectable stars or galactic structure

00:06:45.322 --> 00:06:47.602
despite its substantial hydrogen content.

00:06:47.602 --> 00:06:53.282
Fast sensitivity allowed astronomers to identify the isolated cloud against background emissions.

00:06:53.842 --> 00:06:58.282
The finding adds to ongoing searches for dark galaxies that may contain large amounts

00:06:58.282 --> 00:06:59.382
of unseen matter.

00:06:59.962 --> 00:07:05.202
From rover odometry on Mars to wrinkle ridges on the moon, the past seven days put hard

00:07:05.202 --> 00:07:08.002
numbers on both a driving record and a cooling crust.

00:07:08.782 --> 00:07:14.682
Perseverance added 91 meters in a single day, and with its autonav hazard system has now

00:07:14.682 --> 00:07:17.262
driven farther on Mars than any rover before it.

00:07:17.842 --> 00:07:22.182
Detailed maps of young lunar faults gave the clearest measure yet of how much the moon

00:07:22.182 --> 00:07:23.642
has contracted while cooling.

00:07:24.282 --> 00:07:28.802
Fresh helium measurements confirmed the exact mix forged in the universe's first minutes.

00:07:29.902 --> 00:07:34.962
Seven billion light years away, a supercluster holding more than two quadrillion solar masses

00:07:34.962 --> 00:07:38.802
is being used to test whether dark matter particles interact with one another.

